s by name and party.\nL50: \nL51: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL52: ### In this section\nL53: \nL54:   * 【15†MPs, Lords & offices home】\nL55:   * 【16†MPs†members.parliament.uk】\nL56:   * 【17†Members of the House of Lords†members.parliament.uk】\nL57:   * 【18†Government & opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL58:   * 【19†Parliamentary offices】\nL59:   * 【20†Members' allowances】\nL60:   * 【21†Standards and financial interests】\nL61: ### Members of Parliament\nL62: \nL63: 【22†Find your MP Search for Members by name, postcode, or constituency†members.parliament.uk】 【17†Search for Members of the House of Lords Learn about their experience, knowledge and interests.†members.parliament.uk】\nL64: \nL65: ## About Parliament\nL66: \nL67: Parliament examines what the Government is doing, makes new laws, holds the power to set taxes and debates the issues of the day.\nL68: \nL69: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL70: ### In this section\nL71: \nL72:   * 【23†About Parliament home】\nL73:   * 【24†How Parliament works】\nL74:   * 【25†About MPs, Lords & officers】\nL75:   * 【26†Independent Complaints and Grievance Scheme (ICGS)】\nL76:   * 【27†Living Heritage】\nL77:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL78:   * 【29†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL79:   * 【30†Sustainability and environmental performance in Parliament】\nL80:   * 【31†UK Parliament's Modern Slavery Programme】\nL81: ## Get involved\nL82: \nL83: Read about how to contact an MP or Lord, petition Parliament and find out details of events in your area.\nL84: \nL85: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL86: \nL87: ### In this section\nL88: \nL89:   * 【32†Get involved home】\nL90:   * 【33†Contact an MP or a Member of the Lords】\nL91:   * 【34†Join a learning programme】\nL92:   * 【35†Research impact at the UK Parliament】\nL93:   * 【36†Have your say: Laws and debates】\nL94:   * 【37†Y Gornel Gymraeg】\nL95: ### Get involved with Parliament\nL96: \nL97: 【33†Contact an MP or Lord Contact your MP or a Member of the House of Lords about an issue that matters to you.】 【38†Keep up to date Sign up for the Your Parliament newsletter to find out how you can get involved.†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL98: \nL99: ### It starts with action\nL100: \nL101:   * 【39†Sign or create a petition】\nL102:   * 【40†Sign up to UK Parliament Week†www.ukparliamentweek.org】\nL103: ## Visit Parliament\nL104: \nL105: Find out how to take a tour in person, join an online event, book tickets and plan a visit to UK Parliament.\nL106: \nL107: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL108: \nL109: ### In this section\nL110: \nL111:   * 【41†Visit Parliament home】\nL112:   * 【42†Tours】\nL113:   * 【43†Talks and events】\nL114:   * 【44†Watch debates and committees】\nL115:   * 【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # What do MPs do?\nL156: \nL157: Members of Parliament (MPs) are sent to the House of Commons from every part of the UK to represent local people and ensure they have a voice in national decisions.\nL158: \nL159: MPs work both at the UK Parliament and in their local constituencies. As elected office-holders, there is no statutory job description for MPs and so how they each carry out their role may vary.\nL160: How an MP decides to divide their time can depend upon the schedule of business in the House of Commons, the distance of their constituency from London, their family responsibilities, or whether they have taken on additional responsibilities in the Commons or in the government.\nL161: \nL162: If an MP has taken on an extra role in the House of Commons (such as a committee chair) or in government (such as a ministerial role), they may need to spend more time at Westminster particularly when the House is sitting.\nL163: MPs employ a small number of staff to assist with work at Parliament and help to run offices in the constituency. This allows work to continue in both settings while the MP travels between the two.\nL164: ## MPs at Parliament\nL165: \nL166: In Parliament, MPs are expected to carry out the core work of the House of Commons:\nL167: \nL168:   * 【58†Legislation】 and 【59†taxation】 – examining and approving new laws or taxes\nL169:   * 【60†Scrutiny】 – holding the government to account for its actions\nL170:   * 【61†Debating】 – where competing viewpoints can be expressed and the concerns of constituents can be raised\nL171: \nL172: Find out more at: 【62†What is the role of Parliament?】\nL173: Although each MP is sent from one local area, at Parliament many also consider the best interests of the entire nation.\nL174: MPs carry out their parliamentary work in the debating chambers and committee rooms of the House of Commons, and in their parliamentary offices. Apart from formal parliamentary events, MPs can attend private meetings during each week at Parliament. These may be meetings with representatives from industry, trade unions, campaigners, and lobbyists. They may also attend party meetings and meetings arranged by 【63†All-Party Parliamentary Groups】.\nL175: ## MPs in their constituency\nL176: \nL177: In their constituency, MPs can meet with local businesses and community groups, attend local events and visit schools, hospitals or job centres to see for themselves how services are being delivered. Conversations in their constituency can help to inform an MPs’ priorities when they are in Parliament.\nL178: An MP will often hold advice surgeries, either in person or online, in their constituency. Surgeries are an opportunity for local people to ask if their situation is one that the MP can help them with.\nL179: \nL180: As their representative, an MP is in a position to speak to government officials on behalf of a constituent, or write to the relevant government minister.\nL181: Dealing with cases that are raised by constituents in this way, or via emails and calls to their office, can take up much of an MP’s time when they are not busy with parliamentary work. Constituency office staff can assist with this caseload.\nL182: ## See also\nL183: \nL184:   * 【64†About MPs】\nL185:   * 【65†Raising matters in the House】\nL186:   * 【66†MPs with an additional role】\nL187:   * 【67†MPs' Accountability and Standards】\nL188: \nL189: ## House of Commons Enquiry Service\nL190: \nL191: If you have a question about the work, history or membership of the House of Commons, contact the Enquiry Service via hcenquiries@parliament.uk\nL192: \nL193: 【68†Find out more】\nL194: ## Frequently asked questions: MPs\nL195: \nL196: Read answers to questions that are asked frequently about MPs and their role, with links to additional information.\nL197: \nL198: 【69†Members of Parliament: FAQs】\nL199: \nL200: ## How do I contact my MP?\nL201: \nL202: 【70†How to contact your MP】\nL203: ## Further information\nL204: \nL205: 【71†MPs and constituency etiquette†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL206: \nL207: Commons Library briefing that describes the main conventions followed by MPs when dealing with constituency work.\nL208: \nL209: 【72†A typical day in the life of an MP†www.gov.uk】\nL210: \nL211: The Committee on Standards in Public Life published research to demonstrate a typical day in the life of an MP.\nL212: ###### Popular\nL213: \nL214:   * 【73†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL215:   * 【74†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL216:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL217:   * 【75†Commons Media Relations】\nL218:   * 【76†Lords Press Office】\nL219:   * 【77†Contact us】\nL220: \nL221: ###### Research\nL222: \nL223:   * 【78†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL224:   * 【79†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL225:   * 【80†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL226: \nL227: ###### Search Parliament\nL228: \nL229: [Input]\nL230: \nL231: [Input]\nL232: \nL233: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL234: \nL235: 【81†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL236: ###### Follow Parliament\nL237: \nL238: ###### Legal\nL239: \nL240:   * 【82†Accessibility statement】\nL241:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL242:   * 【83†About this site】\nL243:   * 【84†Privacy notice】\nL244:   * 【85†Cookie policy】\nL245:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL246: \nL247: © UK Parliament 2026\n"
